IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Workers Compensation and Injury Management Act 2023 (Western Australia) -- am I a worker and who is my employer (ss 12, 13,
-- 215, with regs 13-16 and 18); is my employer liable (ss 6, 17, 19, 20); my claim and when liability is taken to be accepted
-- (ss 25, 28, 29, 36, 47, with regs 19 and 22); my income compensation and how long it lasts (ss 46, 48, 51, 55-57, with
-- regs 23 and 24); my medical and health expenses (ss 69, 75-79); who assesses my permanent impairment and who pays (s 186,
-- reg 90); my lump sum for permanent impairment (ss 97, 98, 100, 101, part of the Table); whether my employer must insure
-- me (ss 202, 204); and when I may sue my employer: the 15% whole of person impairment threshold (ss 415, 416, 421, 422),
-- what an election under 25% costs me (s 423), the cap on damages under 25% (s 424, reg 122), and third parties (s 434).
-- The general maximum amount every limit is built on is the s 538 figure as replaced and varied by reg 134.
--
-- SEAT: the injured worker. Every decision is phrased as the worker would ask it: "Am I a worker?", "Is my employer liable
-- to compensate me?", "How much income compensation do I get this week?", "Has my income compensation run out?", "How much
-- can I be paid for medical expenses?", "How much is my lump sum?", "May I start court proceedings against my employer?".
-- Where the Act hands a judgment to an arbitrator, an approved assessor, the Director or a court, the judgment is asked as a
-- fact and the answer is conditional on it.
--
-- Provisions covered: s 6(2), s 12(2), (3), s 13 with regs 13, 15, 16, 18 (and reg 14 as one fact), s 17(1), s 19(1),
-- s 20(2), (3), s 25(1), (5), s 28(1), (4), (6), s 29(1), (3), s 36(1), s 45 ("income compensation general limit amount"),
-- s 46, s 47(2)(a), s 48(2), (3), s 51(1), s 55(2)-(4), s 56, s 57(2), (7), s 69, s 75, s 76, s 78(2), (3), (6), s 97(1)-(3),
-- s 98(2), s 100, s 101(1) and 18 of the 45 Table items, s 186(1), (2), s 202(2), (3), s 204(1), s 215(1), (2), s 411,
-- s 415, s 416(a), (b), (d), s 421(1), (4), (5), s 422(2), s 423, s 424(2), (5), s 434(1), (2), s 538(1), (5), s 566;
-- Workers Compensation and Injury Management Regulations 2024 regs 4, 13-16, 18, 19, 22, 23, 24, 90, 122, 134.
--
-- Edition: compilation from legislation.wa.gov.au (mrdoc 48940, Act No. 21 of 2023, retrieved 7 September 2026), as
-- deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_48940.txt. The deposit names no compilation or reprint number; its
-- compilation table ends with the Workers Compensation and Injury Management Amendment (Common Law Election Validation) Act
-- 2024 (No. 40 of 2024, in operation 30 October 2024). Regulations: Workers Compensation and Injury Management Regulations
-- 2024, compilation version 00-c0-00 current from 30 June 2026 (mrdoc 49606, retrieved 19 October 2026), as deposited at
-- ../../../workers-compensation-and-injury-management-regulations-2024/registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_49606.txt; its
-- compilation table ends with the Amendment Regulations 2026 (SL 2026/35); r 4 of those is uncommenced (1 July 2027) and
-- not shown.
--
-- NUMBERS. The regulations do not state this year's amounts; they state a starting figure for 2024-25 and a method. The
-- general maximum amount (reg 134) and the maximum damages amount for less than 25% impairment (reg 122) are each last
-- year's amount varied by the change in the WA Wage Price Index; the maximum weekly rate of income compensation (reg 23) is
-- twice the average of two ABS average weekly total earnings figures for WA. The index figures are not in the deposit, so
-- they are INPUTS; the method and the starting figures ($252 724 and $530 724) are encoded. Every limit below takes the
-- general maximum amount that applies as an input.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: journeys (s 9), diseases (ss 10, 11, Part 2 Division 9), labour hire (s 14), jockeys (s 15), working
-- directors (s 16), the s 54 pre-injury weekly rate calculation (an INPUT), the s 55(5) indexation, the s 57(3) minimum
-- amount (an INPUT), s 58 board and lodging, additional income compensation (s 52), suspension and discontinuance (Part 2
-- Division 3 Subdivision 4), miscellaneous and rehabilitation expenses (Divisions 5, 6), noise-induced hearing loss, dust
-- disease, death, settlement agreements (Division 12; their registration is an INPUT), injury management (Part 3),
-- dispute resolution (Part 6), double recovery (Part 7 Division 3), choice of law (Part 12), and administration. Schedule 2
-- of the regulations (religious workers) is not read: the class is one fact.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill, from the seat of the
-- individual to whom the law is applied, with the design defaults ruled on 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Workers Compensation and Injury Management Act 2023 (WA) -- the injured worker`

§§ `538, 566 and regs 4, 23, 122, 134 -- the amounts`

-- s 538(1): "the general maximum amount is $243 991". s 566: if the prescribed amount under the former Act immediately before
-- commencement day differs, it "replaces the amount of $243 991". Reg 134 note 2: "The amount of $252 724 is the prescribed
-- amount before commencement which replaces the amount of $243 991".
-- Reg 134: the general maximum amount "must be calculated by -- (a) for the financial year commencing on 1 July 2024 --
-- varying the amount of $252 724 by the percentage by which the WPI varied ...; or (b) for ... each financial year after that
-- -- varying the general maximum amount for the previous financial year by the percentage by which the WPI varied".
-- Reg 122: the same method for the maximum damages amount for less than 25% impairment, starting from "$530 724".
-- s 538(5): "If, for a particular period, variation under the regulations of an adjustable amount would reduce the amount, the
-- amount must not be varied for the period."
-- Reg 4: amounts "must be rounded to the nearest whole dollar with an amount that is 50 cents more than a whole dollar being
-- rounded up".
`the general maximum amount before the 2024-25 variation` MEANS 252724

`the maximum damages amount before the 2024-25 variation` MEANS 530724

@export Round an amount to the nearest whole dollar, 50 cents up (Workers Compensation and Injury Management Regulations 2024 reg 4)
GIVEN `amount` IS A NUMBER @desc An amount worked out under the regulations
GIVETH A NUMBER
`rounded to the nearest whole dollar` MEANS FLOOR (`amount` PLUS 0.5)

@export What is this year's amount, varied by the WA Wage Price Index (regs 122 and 134; Act s 538(5))?
GIVEN `last year's amount` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount for the previous financial year (for 2024-25, the starting figure in the regulation)
      `the WPI change in per cent` IS A NUMBER @desc The percentage by which the WA Wage Price Index varied between the last two December quarters before the year
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the amount varied by the WPI` MEANS
    IF `the WPI change in per cent` AT MOST 0
    THEN `last year's amount`
    ELSE `rounded to the nearest whole dollar` (`last year's amount` TIMES (100 PLUS `the WPI change in per cent`) DIVIDED BY 100)

-- Reg 23: "the maximum weekly rate of income compensation for a financial year must be calculated by multiplying by 2 the
-- average of the amounts that the Australian Bureau of Statistics published as the all employees average weekly total
-- earnings in Western Australia for pay periods ending in the months of May and November preceding the financial year."
-- s 538(5), as above: the rate is never varied downwards.
@export What is the most I can be paid in income compensation a week this year (Act s 53, s 56; reg 23)?
GIVEN `the May average weekly total earnings` IS A NUMBER @desc ABS all employees average weekly total earnings, WA, for pay periods ending in May before the year
      `the November average weekly total earnings` IS A NUMBER @desc The same figure for pay periods ending in November before the year
      `last year's maximum weekly rate` IS A NUMBER @desc The maximum weekly rate for the previous financial year
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum weekly rate of income compensation` MEANS
    max `last year's maximum weekly rate`
        (`rounded to the nearest whole dollar`
            ((`the May average weekly total earnings` PLUS `the November average weekly total earnings`) DIVIDED BY 2 TIMES 2))

§§ `12, 13 and regs 13-18 -- am I a worker`

-- s 12(2): "An individual is a worker if -- (a) the individual has entered into, or works under, a contract of service with a
-- person ...; or (b) [an apprentice under a contract and a registered training contract]; or (c) the individual has contracted
-- with a person for the performance of work by the individual and -- (i) the work is not work in the course of or incidental to
-- a trade or business regularly carried on by the individual in the individual's own name or under a business or firm name; and
-- (ii) the individual does not sublet the contract; and (iii) if the individual employs a worker, the individual performs part
-- of the work personally."
-- Whether the work is in the course of a business I regularly carry on in my own name is a question of fact: asked, not worked
-- out.
DECLARE `My engagement` HAS
    `I have a contract of service`                                                     IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 12(2)(a)
    `I am an apprentice under a contract and a registered training contract`           IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 12(2)(b)
    `I contracted to do the work myself`                                               IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 12(2)(c)
    `the work is part of a trade or business I regularly carry on in my own or a business name` IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 12(2)(c)(i)
    `I sublet the contract`                                                            IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 12(2)(c)(ii)
    `I employ a worker`                                                                IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 12(2)(c)(iii)
    `I do part of the work personally`                                                 IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 12(2)(c)(iii)

-- s 13: regulations may make an individual of a class a worker (s 13(1)) or not a worker (s 13(3)).
-- Reg 13 Table: holders of judicial and statutory office, members of the Governor's Establishment and parliamentary staff "is a
-- worker". Reg 14: a religious worker of a class in Schedule 2 is a worker (Schedule 2 not read: one fact). Reg 15(2): a person
-- engaged to deliver a funded support by an NDIS participant whose funding is self-managed or plan-managed "is a worker" -- but
-- not (reg 15(3)) one employed by a business that supplies support workers and contracts to provide the support. Reg 16(2): a
-- police officer or Aboriginal police liaison officer "is not a worker unless [he or she] dies as a result of an injury".
-- Reg 18: "A person who is a sporting contestant under contract is not a worker" when engaged in contests, training, promotion
-- or travel for them.
DECLARE `Prescribed class` IS ONE OF
    `none of the prescribed classes`
    `a holder of an office in the regulation 13 Table`
    `a religious worker of a class in Schedule 2`
    `an NDIS support worker engaged by a self-managing or plan-managed participant`
    `an NDIS support worker employed by a business that supplies support workers`
    `a police officer or Aboriginal police liaison officer`
    `a sporting contestant under contract`

GIVEN `the engagement` IS A `My engagement`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`I am a worker under section 12(2)` MEANS
        `the engagement`'s `I have a contract of service`
    OR  `the engagement`'s `I am an apprentice under a contract and a registered training contract`
    OR  (    `the engagement`'s `I contracted to do the work myself`
         AND (NOT `the engagement`'s `the work is part of a trade or business I regularly carry on in my own or a business name`)
         AND (NOT `the engagement`'s `I sublet the contract`)
         AND ((NOT `the engagement`'s `I employ a worker`) OR `the engagement`'s `I do part of the work personally`))

@export Am I a worker under the Workers Compensation and Injury Management Act 2023 (WA) (s 12, s 13; regs 13-18)?
GIVEN `the engagement` IS A `My engagement` @desc How you are engaged to do the work
      `my class` IS A `Prescribed class` @desc Whether you fall in a class the regulations make a worker or not a worker
      `I died as a result of the injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only matters for a police officer or Aboriginal police liaison officer
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I a worker` IF
    CONSIDER `my class`
    WHEN `a holder of an office in the regulation 13 Table` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a religious worker of a class in Schedule 2` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an NDIS support worker engaged by a self-managing or plan-managed participant` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a police officer or Aboriginal police liaison officer` THEN `I died as a result of the injury`
    WHEN `a sporting contestant under contract` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE `I am a worker under section 12(2)` `the engagement`

-- s 12(3): "The person with whom the worker has entered into the contract, or for whom the worker works under the contract, is
-- the worker's employer." A person with no contract with me -- a tenant of the building where I work, a driver on the road --
-- is not my employer under s 12.
@export Is this person my employer (s 12(3))?
GIVEN `I am a worker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a worker
      `the person is the other party to my contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you contracted with the person, or work for the person under the contract
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is this person my employer` IF
    `I am a worker` AND `the person is the other party to my contract`

-- s 215(1): if a worker is injured in relevant employment with a contractor, "both the principal and the contractor are ... taken
-- to be employers of the worker" and are "jointly and severally liable". s 215(2): relevant employment if "(a) the employment is
-- in performance of any work by or under the contractor; and (b) the work on which the worker is employed is directly a part of
-- or process in the trade or business of the principal; and (c) the injury arises in respect of premises on which the principal
-- has undertaken to do the work or that are otherwise under the control or management of the principal." s 214: a principal is
-- "a person who contracts with a contractor for the execution of any work".
@export Is this person also taken to be my employer, as the principal who contracted my employer (s 215)?
GIVEN `the person contracted my employer for the work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person contracted your employer to do the work
      `I was employed in that work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were employed in performing that work
      `the work is directly part of the person's trade or business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the work is directly a part of or process in the person's trade or business
      `the premises are under the person's control or management` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury arose on premises where the person undertook the work or that it controls or manages
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is this person also my employer as principal` IF
        `the person contracted my employer for the work`
    AND `I was employed in that work`
    AND `the work is directly part of the person's trade or business`
    AND `the premises are under the person's control or management`

§§ `6, 17, 19, 20 -- is my employer liable`

-- s 6(2): "A personal injury by accident is an injury from employment if the injury arises out of or in the course of the
-- employment or while the worker is acting under the employer's instructions."
-- s 17(1): "An employer is liable for compensation if a worker suffers an injury from employment with the employer."
-- s 19(1): "Liability for compensation arises only if the worker's employment is connected with this State."
-- s 20(2): no liability if it is "proved before an arbitrator" that the injury is attributable to impairing alcohol or drugs,
-- failure to use protective equipment provided, or "other serious and wilful misconduct"; s 20(3): this "does not exclude
-- liability if the worker's injury has serious and permanent effects on the worker or results in the death of the worker".
@export Is my personal injury by accident an injury from employment (s 6(2))?
GIVEN `it arose out of or in the course of my employment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury arose out of or in the course of your employment
      `I was acting under my employer's instructions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were acting under your employer's instructions
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my injury an injury from employment` IF
    `it arose out of or in the course of my employment` OR `I was acting under my employer's instructions`

@export Is my employer liable to compensate me (ss 17, 19, 20)?
GIVEN `I am a worker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a worker
      `an injury from employment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your injury is an injury from employment with this employer
      `my employment is connected with WA` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your employment is connected with Western Australia
      `an arbitrator found the injury attributable to my misconduct under s 20` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was proved before an arbitrator that the injury came from impairing alcohol or drugs, not using protective equipment provided, or serious and wilful misconduct
      `serious and permanent effects` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury has serious and permanent effects on you
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my employer liable to compensate me` IF
        `I am a worker`
    AND `an injury from employment`
    AND `my employment is connected with WA`
    AND ((NOT `an arbitrator found the injury attributable to my misconduct under s 20`) OR `serious and permanent effects`)

§§ `25, 28, 29, 36, 47 and regs 19, 22 -- my claim`

-- s 25(1): "A claim for compensation must be made within 12 months after the injury occurs." s 25(5): a failure to claim in time
-- "does not invalidate the claim if -- (a) the failure ... results from mistake, absence from the State or another reasonable
-- cause; or (b) the failure ... would not prejudice the employer's defence".
GIVEN `from` IS A DATE
      `to` IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`completed months from` MEANS
    (DATE_YEAR `to` MINUS DATE_YEAR `from`) TIMES 12
    PLUS (DATE_MONTH `to` MINUS DATE_MONTH `from`)
    MINUS (IF DATE_DAY `to` LESS THAN DATE_DAY `from` THEN 1 ELSE 0)

@export Is my claim made within 12 months after my injury (s 25(1))?
GIVEN `the day of my injury` IS A DATE @desc The day the injury occurred
      `the day I made my claim` IS A DATE @desc The day you gave your employer the claim form and certificate of capacity
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my claim made within 12 months` IF
        `completed months from` `the day of my injury` `the day I made my claim` LESS THAN 12
    OR  (    `completed months from` `the day of my injury` `the day I made my claim` EQUALS 12
         AND DATE_DAY `the day I made my claim` EQUALS DATE_DAY `the day of my injury`)

@export Does the timing of my claim leave it valid (s 25(1), (5))?
GIVEN `the day of my injury` IS A DATE @desc The day the injury occurred
      `the day I made my claim` IS A DATE @desc The day you gave your employer the claim form and certificate of capacity
      `the lateness came from mistake, absence from WA or another reasonable cause` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a late claim results from mistake, absence from the State or another reasonable cause
      `the lateness would not prejudice my employer's defence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a late claim would not prejudice your employer's defence
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my claim valid as to time` IF
        `Is my claim made within 12 months` `the day of my injury` `the day I made my claim`
    OR  `the lateness came from mistake, absence from WA or another reasonable cause`
    OR  `the lateness would not prejudice my employer's defence`

-- s 28(1): "Within 14 days after a claim is given to an insurer ... the insurer ... must give the worker ... a liability decision
-- notice"; s 28(4): or, within the same 14 days, "a deferred decision notice"; s 28(6): if neither is given "as and when
-- required", the insurer "is taken to have accepted that the employer is liable".
-- s 29(1), (3): after a deferred decision notice, a liability decision notice must come "before the day prescribed by the
-- regulations ... (the deemed liability acceptance day)", or liability is taken to be accepted.
-- Reg 19: the deemed liability acceptance day "is the day after the period of 120 days beginning on the day on which the insurer
-- ... receives the claim". Counting the day of receipt as day 0, the period is days 0 to 119 and the deemed day is day 120.
-- s 36(1) and reg 22: if a deferred decision notice was given and no liability decision notice comes before the provisional
-- payments day -- "the day after the period of 28 days beginning on the day on which the insurer ... receives the claim", day 28
-- on the same count -- "the employer is required to make provisional payments".
@export Is my employer's insurer taken to have accepted liability for my claim (ss 28(6), 29(3); reg 19)?
GIVEN `a liability decision notice has been given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer has given you a liability decision notice (accepting or not)
      `a deferred decision notice was given within 14 days` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer gave you a deferred decision notice within 14 days after receiving the claim
      `days since the insurer received my claim` IS A NUMBER @desc Days since the insurer received the claim, the day of receipt being day 0
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is liability taken to be accepted` IF
        (NOT `a liability decision notice has been given`)
    AND (    ((NOT `a deferred decision notice was given within 14 days`) AND `days since the insurer received my claim` GREATER THAN 14)
         OR  (`a deferred decision notice was given within 14 days` AND `days since the insurer received my claim` AT LEAST 120))

@export Must my employer make provisional payments to me (s 36(1); reg 22)?
GIVEN `a deferred decision notice was given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer gave a deferred decision notice
      `a liability decision notice has been given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer has since given a liability decision notice
      `days since the insurer received my claim` IS A NUMBER @desc Days since the insurer received the claim, the day of receipt being day 0
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must my employer make provisional payments` IF
        `a deferred decision notice was given`
    AND (NOT `a liability decision notice has been given`)
    AND `days since the insurer received my claim` AT LEAST 28

-- s 47(2)(a): the employer must "make the first payment of income compensation within 14 days after the employer's obligation to
-- pay income compensation arises", including payments accrued from the first day of incapacity. Penalty: "a fine of $5 000".
@export Is my first payment of income compensation overdue (s 47(2)(a))?
GIVEN `days since the obligation to pay me arose` IS A NUMBER @desc Days since liability for income compensation was accepted, taken to be accepted, or determined
      `I have been paid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the first payment has been made
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my first payment overdue` IF
    (NOT `I have been paid`) AND `days since the obligation to pay me arose` GREATER THAN 14

§§ `46, 48, 51, 55, 56, 57 and reg 24 -- my income compensation`

-- s 57(7): "No minimum weekly rate applies under this section if -- (a) the only component of the worker's preinjury weekly rate
-- of income is the monetary value of the provision of board and lodging; or (b) the worker is a working director of a company; or
-- (c) the worker is a jockey; or (d) the worker is a worker of a class prescribed by the regulations".
-- Reg 24: "No minimum weekly rate applies under section 57 if the worker is -- (a) a worker to whom no industrial instrument
-- applies; or (b) a worker to whom no minimum amount applies under the Minimum Conditions of Employment Act 1993."
@export Is there a minimum weekly rate of income compensation for me (s 57(7); reg 24)?
GIVEN `an industrial instrument applies to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an award, industrial agreement or fair work instrument applies to you
      `a minimum amount applies to me under the Minimum Conditions of Employment Act 1993` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that Act sets a minimum amount for you
      `my earnings are only board and lodging` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether board and lodging is the only part of your pre-injury earnings
      `I am a working director or a jockey` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a working director of a company or a jockey
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is there a minimum weekly rate for me` IF
        `an industrial instrument applies to me`
    AND `a minimum amount applies to me under the Minimum Conditions of Employment Act 1993`
    AND (NOT `my earnings are only board and lodging`)
    AND (NOT `I am a working director or a jockey`)

-- s 46: "A worker is entitled to be paid income compensation ... if the worker's injury results in total or partial incapacity".
-- s 55(2): for "the first 26 weeks in which income compensation is payable", total incapacity is paid "at the worker's preinjury
-- weekly rate of income"; s 55(3): after that, "at 85% of the worker's preinjury weekly rate of income", in each case "except as
-- otherwise provided in section 56 or 57". s 55(4)(b): a week counts if compensation "is payable for any day or days during the
-- week". s 56: a payment above the maximum weekly rate "must be reduced" to it. s 57(2): one below the minimum weekly rate is "to
-- be increased" to it (pass 0 where there is none). s 48(2): for partial incapacity, the total-incapacity amount less "the
-- amount the worker earns, or is able to earn, in suitable employment"; s 48(3): nothing if that is equal or greater.
@export How much income compensation do I get for this week (ss 48, 55, 56, 57)?
GIVEN `my preinjury weekly rate of income` IS A NUMBER @desc Your average weekly earnings over the year before the injury (s 54)
      `which week of compensation this is` IS A NUMBER @desc 1 for the first week in which income compensation is payable, and so on
      `the maximum weekly rate` IS A NUMBER @desc The maximum weekly rate of income compensation for the year (reg 23)
      `the minimum weekly rate` IS A NUMBER @desc Your minimum weekly rate under s 57(3), or 0 if none applies
      `what I earn or can earn in suitable employment` IS A NUMBER @desc For partial incapacity, what you earn or are able to earn a week in suitable employment; 0 if totally incapacitated
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much income compensation do I get this week` MEANS
    max 0 (`the total incapacity amount` MINUS `what I earn or can earn in suitable employment`)
    WHERE
        `the section 55 amount` MEANS
            IF `which week of compensation this is` AT MOST 26
            THEN `my preinjury weekly rate of income`
            ELSE `my preinjury weekly rate of income` TIMES 85 DIVIDED BY 100
        `the total incapacity amount` MEANS
            IF `the section 55 amount` GREATER THAN `the maximum weekly rate`
            THEN `the maximum weekly rate`
            ELSE max `the section 55 amount` `the minimum weekly rate`

-- The total for an unbroken run of weeks of total incapacity from the first week: the weeks within the first 26 at the full rate,
-- the rest at 85%, each capped at the maximum.
@export How much income compensation do I get for my first weeks off work, totally incapacitated (ss 55, 56)?
GIVEN `my preinjury weekly rate of income` IS A NUMBER @desc Your average weekly earnings over the year before the injury (s 54)
      `weeks off` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of weeks in which income compensation is payable, counted from the first
      `the maximum weekly rate` IS A NUMBER @desc The maximum weekly rate of income compensation for the year (reg 23)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much income compensation do I get for my first weeks off` MEANS
        (min `weeks off` 26 TIMES `How much income compensation do I get this week` `my preinjury weekly rate of income` 1 `the maximum weekly rate` 0 0)
    PLUS (max (`weeks off` MINUS 26) 0 TIMES `How much income compensation do I get this week` `my preinjury weekly rate of income` 27 `the maximum weekly rate` 0 0)

-- s 51(1): entitlement "ceases when the total of all amounts of income compensation paid to the worker for that incapacity reaches
-- the income compensation general limit amount", which s 45 makes "equal to the general maximum amount".
@export Has my income compensation run out (s 51(1))?
GIVEN `income compensation paid to me so far` IS A NUMBER @desc The total income compensation paid for this incapacity
      `the general maximum amount` IS A NUMBER @desc The general maximum amount that applies (s 538; reg 134)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Has my income compensation run out` IF
    `income compensation paid to me so far` AT LEAST `the general maximum amount`

§§ `69, 75, 76, 78 -- my medical and health expenses`

-- s 69: "medical and health expenses general limit amount means the amount that is 60%, or a greater percentage, if any,
-- prescribed by the regulations, of the general maximum amount"; "standard increase limit amount" is "40% ... of the medical and
-- health expenses general limit amount"; "special increase limit amount" is "190% ... of" it. The deposited regulations prescribe
-- no greater percentage (none found in Part 4 Division 4).
-- s 75: the total "must not exceed the medical and health expenses general limit amount".
@export How much can I be paid in total for medical and health expenses (ss 69, 75)?
GIVEN `the general maximum amount` IS A NUMBER @desc The general maximum amount that applies (s 538; reg 134)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much can I be paid for medical and health expenses` MEANS
    `the general maximum amount` TIMES 60 DIVIDED BY 100

@export By how much more can an arbitrator raise my medical expenses limit by standard increases (ss 69, 77)?
GIVEN `the general maximum amount` IS A NUMBER @desc The general maximum amount that applies (s 538; reg 134)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the standard increase limit amount` MEANS
    `How much can I be paid for medical and health expenses` `the general maximum amount` TIMES 40 DIVIDED BY 100

@export By how much more can an arbitrator raise my medical expenses limit by special increases (ss 69, 78)?
GIVEN `the general maximum amount` IS A NUMBER @desc The general maximum amount that applies (s 538; reg 134)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the special increase limit amount` MEANS
    `How much can I be paid for medical and health expenses` `the general maximum amount` TIMES 190 DIVIDED BY 100

-- s 76: "When the total amount of medical and health expenses compensation paid ... reaches 60% of the medical and health
-- expenses general limit amount", the employer or insurer "must within 14 days give the worker notice of that fact".
@export Must I be told that 60% of my medical expenses limit has been used (s 76)?
GIVEN `medical expenses paid so far` IS A NUMBER @desc The total medical and health expenses compensation paid for the injury
      `the general maximum amount` IS A NUMBER @desc The general maximum amount that applies (s 538; reg 134)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I be told 60% of my medical limit is used` IF
    `medical expenses paid so far` AT LEAST `How much can I be paid for medical and health expenses` `the general maximum amount` TIMES 60 DIVIDED BY 100

-- s 78(2): an application "may be made only if -- (a) the general limit for the claim has been increased by a standard increase;
-- and (b) ... reasonable special expenses in excess of those that can be provided for by a standard increase". s 78(3): the
-- arbitrator may order it if "(a) the worker has a degree of permanent whole of person impairment of at least 15%" (as determined
-- under s 79), and is satisfied of the expenses, of the regulations' matters (reg 33: that operative intervention is required),
-- and of the worker's needs. s 78(6): not "more than 5 years after the relevant determination of liability".
@export May an arbitrator give me a special increase in my medical expenses limit (s 78)?
GIVEN `I have had a standard increase` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your limit has already been raised by a standard increase
      `my whole of person impairment` IS A NUMBER @desc Your degree of permanent whole of person impairment, in per cent, as determined under s 79
      `my special expenses exceed what a standard increase covers` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your reasonable special expenses exceed what a standard increase can provide
      `the arbitrator is satisfied of the rest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the arbitrator is satisfied of the reg 33 matters and of your needs
      `within 5 years of the determination of liability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you apply within 5 years after liability was accepted or determined
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I get a special increase` IF
        `I have had a standard increase`
    AND `my whole of person impairment` AT LEAST 15
    AND `my special expenses exceed what a standard increase covers`
    AND `the arbitrator is satisfied of the rest`
    AND `within 5 years of the determination of liability`

§§ `186 and reg 90 -- who assesses my impairment, and who pays`

-- s 186(1): "The assessment of a worker's degree of permanent impairment must be made by an approved permanent impairment assessor
-- except as provided by subsection (2)"; s 186(2): for a dust disease, "by a Dust Disease Medical Panel".
DECLARE Assessor IS ONE OF
    `an approved permanent impairment assessor`
    `a Dust Disease Medical Panel`
    `my treating doctor, not approved as an assessor`
    `the insurer's doctor, not approved as an assessor`

@export Is my permanent impairment assessed by someone the Act allows (s 186)?
GIVEN `the assessor` IS AN Assessor @desc Who made the assessment
      `a dust disease` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury is a dust disease
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my assessment made by someone the Act allows` IF
    CONSIDER `the assessor`
    WHEN `an approved permanent impairment assessor` THEN NOT `a dust disease`
    WHEN `a Dust Disease Medical Panel` THEN `a dust disease`
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- Reg 89: an assessment may be requested by "(a) the worker; or (b) the insurer or self-insurer". Reg 90(1): if the insurer
-- requests it, "the cost ... must be paid for by whichever made the request"; reg 90(2): if the worker requests it "and the
-- assessment is not a miscellaneous expense under section 91 of the Act, the cost ... must be paid for by the worker".
DECLARE `Who requested the assessment` IS ONE OF
    `I did`
    `the insurer or self-insurer did`

DECLARE `Who pays for the assessment` IS ONE OF
    `the insurer or self-insurer that asked for it`
    `I do`
    `it is compensated as a miscellaneous expense under s 91, which this encoding does not cover`

@export Who pays for my permanent impairment assessment (reg 90)?
GIVEN `the requester` IS A `Who requested the assessment` @desc Who asked for the assessment
      `it is a miscellaneous expense under s 91` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the assessment is a miscellaneous expense under s 91 of the Act
GIVETH A `Who pays for the assessment`
DECIDE `Who pays for my assessment` IS
    CONSIDER `the requester`
    WHEN `the insurer or self-insurer did` THEN `the insurer or self-insurer that asked for it`
    WHEN `I did` THEN
        IF `it is a miscellaneous expense under s 91`
        THEN `it is compensated as a miscellaneous expense under s 91, which this encoding does not cover`
        ELSE `I do`

§§ `97, 98, 100, 101 -- my lump sum for permanent impairment`

-- s 97(1): payable "as a lump sum amount for permanent impairment ... as a result of an injury that is personal injury by
-- accident"; s 97(2): "only for permanent impairment of a kind described in column 1 of the Table to section 101".
-- s 101(1)(b): for less than 100%, "the amount calculated by multiplying the percentage degree of permanent impairment by the
-- percentage of the lump sum limit that is indicated for the impairment in column 2 of the Table"; s 100(1) and s 101(2): the
-- lump sum limit is the general maximum amount "applying on the day on which the injury occurred".
-- s 98(2): the degree is "(a) ... of that part or faculty of the body" -- not the whole person -- or "(b) in the case of impairment
-- from scarring or disfigurement, the degree of permanent whole of person impairment".
-- 18 of the 45 Table items are encoded (item number and column 2 percentage in the comment).
DECLARE `Table item` IS ONE OF
    `impairment of sight of 1 eye`                                          -- item 3: 50
    `impairment of hearing`                                                 -- item 5: 75
    `impairment of arm at or above elbow`                                   -- item 12: 90
    `impairment of arm below elbow`                                         -- item 13: 80
    `impairment of hand or thumb and 4 fingers`                             -- item 16: 80
    `impairment of thumb`                                                   -- item 17: 35
    `impairment of leg at or above knee`                                    -- item 18: 70
    `impairment of leg below knee`                                          -- item 31: 65
    `impairment of foot`                                                    -- item 33: 65
    `impairment of great toe`                                               -- item 34: 20
    `impairment of the back (thoracic spine or lumbar spine or both)`       -- item 39: 75
    `impairment of the neck (including cervical spine)`                     -- item 40: 55
    `impairment of the pelvis`                                              -- item 41: 30
    `impairment of mental capacity`                                         -- item 7: 100
    `impairment of spinal cord function`                                    -- item 8: 100
    `impairment of sense of taste and smell`                                -- item 9: 50
    `impairment from facial scarring or disfigurement`                      -- Miscellaneous: 80
    `impairment from bodily, other than facial, scarring or disfigurement`  -- Miscellaneous: 50

GIVEN `the item` IS A `Table item`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the Table percentage` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the item`
    WHEN `impairment of sight of 1 eye` THEN 50
    WHEN `impairment of hearing` THEN 75
    WHEN `impairment of arm at or above elbow` THEN 90
    WHEN `impairment of arm below elbow` THEN 80
    WHEN `impairment of hand or thumb and 4 fingers` THEN 80
    WHEN `impairment of thumb` THEN 35
    WHEN `impairment of leg at or above knee` THEN 70
    WHEN `impairment of leg below knee` THEN 65
    WHEN `impairment of foot` THEN 65
    WHEN `impairment of great toe` THEN 20
    WHEN `impairment of the back (thoracic spine or lumbar spine or both)` THEN 75
    WHEN `impairment of the neck (including cervical spine)` THEN 55
    WHEN `impairment of the pelvis` THEN 30
    WHEN `impairment of mental capacity` THEN 100
    WHEN `impairment of spinal cord function` THEN 100
    WHEN `impairment of sense of taste and smell` THEN 50
    WHEN `impairment from facial scarring or disfigurement` THEN 80
    WHEN `impairment from bodily, other than facial, scarring or disfigurement` THEN 50

@export How much is my lump sum for this permanent impairment (ss 98, 100, 101)?
GIVEN `the item` IS A `Table item` @desc The kind of permanent impairment, as described in the Table to s 101
      `my degree of permanent impairment` IS A NUMBER @desc The degree, in per cent, of impairment of that part or faculty of the body (for scarring, of the whole person)
      `the general maximum amount on the day of my injury` IS A NUMBER @desc The general maximum amount applying on the day of the injury (s 538; reg 134)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much is my lump sum` MEANS
    `the general maximum amount on the day of my injury` TIMES `the Table percentage` `the item` DIVIDED BY 100 TIMES `my degree of permanent impairment` DIVIDED BY 100

-- s 100(2): with more than one impairment from an injury, the limit "applies as a limit on the aggregate".
@export How much is my lump sum for all my impairments from one injury (s 100)?
GIVEN `the sum of the separate amounts` IS A NUMBER @desc The lump sums worked out for each impairment, added up
      `the general maximum amount on the day of my injury` IS A NUMBER @desc The general maximum amount applying on the day of the injury
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much is my total lump sum` MEANS
    min `the sum of the separate amounts` `the general maximum amount on the day of my injury`

-- s 97(3): "payable only when the employer's liability to the worker for compensation for the injury is commuted by a settlement
-- agreement registered under Division 12". s 423(1)(c): after a registered election to retain the right to seek damages, with
-- whole of person impairment assessed under 25%, "the worker is not entitled to any permanent impairment compensation".
@export Is my lump sum for permanent impairment payable (ss 97, 423(1)(c))?
GIVEN `a personal injury by accident` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury is a personal injury by accident
      `my liability is commuted by a registered settlement agreement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your employer's liability has been commuted by a settlement agreement registered under Division 12
      `I have a registered election to seek damages` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your election to retain the right to seek damages has been registered
      `my assessed whole of person impairment` IS A NUMBER @desc Your degree of permanent whole of person impairment, in per cent, as assessed by an approved assessor
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my lump sum payable` IF
        `a personal injury by accident`
    AND `my liability is commuted by a registered settlement agreement`
    AND ((NOT `I have a registered election to seek damages`) OR `my assessed whole of person impairment` AT LEAST 25)

§§ `202, 204 -- must my employer insure me`

-- s 202(2): "An employer must at all times have a current workers compensation policy issued by a licensed insurer." s 202(3): not
-- "while the employer is a selfinsurer". There is no minimum number of workers or payroll.
-- s 204(1): failure is an offence; "Penalty for this subsection: a fine of $10 000 in respect of each of the employer's workers to
-- whom the offence relates." s 31(1): if the employer is uninsured, a claim proceeds as if the employer were a self-insurer.
@export Must my employer hold a current workers compensation policy covering me (s 202)?
GIVEN `my employer is a self-insurer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your employer is a licensed self-insurer
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must my employer insure me` IF
    NOT `my employer is a self-insurer`

@export What is the most my employer can be fined for not insuring its workers (s 204(1))?
GIVEN `number of workers` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of the employer's workers the failure relates to
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the most my employer can be fined for being uninsured` MEANS
    10000 TIMES `number of workers`

§§ `411, 415, 416, 421, 422, 424, 434 -- when may I sue`

-- s 411: "third party means a person other than the employer".
-- s 415: Division 2 "applies to an award of damages against a worker's employer independently of this Act ... if -- (a) the injury
-- was caused by the tort of the employer; and (b) compensation for the injury has been paid or is payable".
-- s 416: it "does not apply to -- (a) an award of damages in respect of an injury that results in the death of the worker; or (b) an
-- award of damages to which the Motor Vehicle (Third Party Insurance) Act 1943 applies; ... (d) an award of damages against a person
-- who is a worker's employer (a deemed employer) only because of section 215 or 225".
DECLARE Defendant IS ONE OF
    `my employer`
    `a person who is my employer only because of s 215`
    `a third party`

@export Does the 15% threshold and the rest of Part 7 Division 2 apply to my claim for damages against this person (ss 415, 416)?
GIVEN `the defendant` IS A Defendant @desc Whom you would sue
      `compensation is paid or payable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether workers compensation for the injury has been paid or is payable
      `the injury caused my death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury results in death
      `the Motor Vehicle (Third Party Insurance) Act 1943 applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the damages are a motor vehicle third party insurance claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does Division 2 apply to my claim` IF
        `the defendant` EQUALS `my employer`
    AND `compensation is paid or payable`
    AND (NOT `the injury caused my death`)
    AND (NOT `the Motor Vehicle (Third Party Insurance) Act 1943 applies`)

-- s 421(1): proceedings against the employer "must not be commenced" unless "(a) the worker's degree of permanent whole of person
-- impairment resulting from the injury has been assessed to be at least 15% and that assessment has been recorded by the Director
-- as the supporting assessment ...; (b) the worker has elected ... to retain the right to seek the damages and the Director has --
-- (i) registered the election ...; and (ii) notified the worker in writing". Reg 120: the election is lodged with the Director on
-- the approved form with the supporting assessment; reg 121: the Director registers it and notifies the worker and the employer.
-- s 422(2): a worker with a terminal disease (s 422(1)) may commence first.
@export May I start court proceedings for damages against this person (ss 421(1), 422(2); regs 120, 121)?
GIVEN `Division 2 applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether Part 7 Division 2 applies to the claim
      `my assessed whole of person impairment` IS A NUMBER @desc Your degree of permanent whole of person impairment, in per cent, in the supporting assessment
      `my election is registered and I have been notified` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Director has registered your election and notified you in writing
      `I have a terminal disease under s 422` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a respiratory specialist has diagnosed a s 422 disease and certified death likely within 2 years
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I start court proceedings` IF
        (NOT `Division 2 applies`)
    OR  `I have a terminal disease under s 422`
    OR  (`my assessed whole of person impairment` AT LEAST 15 AND `my election is registered and I have been notified`)

-- s 421(4)(b): no award against the employer unless "the court is satisfied that the worker's degree of permanent whole of person
-- impairment resulting from the injury is at least 15%"; s 421(5): the court "is not bound by the supporting assessment".
@export Can the court award me damages against this person (s 421(4), (5))?
GIVEN `Division 2 applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether Part 7 Division 2 applies to the claim
      `the impairment the court finds` IS A NUMBER @desc The degree of permanent whole of person impairment, in per cent, the court is satisfied of
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the court award me damages` IF
    (NOT `Division 2 applies`) OR `the impairment the court finds` AT LEAST 15

-- s 424(2): "Unless the court is satisfied that the worker's degree of permanent whole of person impairment is at least 25%", the
-- maximum award against the employer is the prescribed "maximum damages amount for less than 25% impairment" (reg 122, above),
-- awarded "only in a most extreme case"; otherwise "a proportion, determined according to the severity". s 424(3): the amount "at
-- the time the award is made". s 424(5): no maximum at 25% or more.
@export Are my damages against my employer capped (s 424)?
GIVEN `Division 2 applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether Part 7 Division 2 applies to the claim
      `the impairment the court finds` IS A NUMBER @desc The degree of permanent whole of person impairment, in per cent, the court is satisfied of
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Are my damages capped` IF
    `Division 2 applies` AND `the impairment the court finds` LESS THAN 25

-- s 434(1): "When a third party is liable to pay damages in respect of an injury for which compensation is payable, the worker may
-- take proceedings both against the third party to recover damages and against any person liable to pay compensation"; s 434(2):
-- the worker is not "entitled to recover both damages and compensation" -- damages recovered are brought to account.
@export May I sue a third party for damages while I claim compensation (s 434)?
GIVEN `the third party is liable to pay me damages` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a person other than your employer is liable in damages for the injury (a question for the general law)
      `compensation is payable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether workers compensation is payable for the injury
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I sue a third party as well` IF
    `the third party is liable to pay me damages` AND `compensation is payable`

§§ `423 -- what an election under 25% costs me`

-- s 423(1): once the election is registered and the whole of person impairment "as assessed by an approved permanent impairment
-- assessor is less than 25%": income compensation for "the first 6 months after the day on which the election is registered" is
-- reduced; none "for any time that is more than 6 months after the registration day"; no permanent impairment compensation; and no
-- medical, miscellaneous or rehabilitation expenses compensation "for expenses incurred after the registration day".
-- s 423(2): "70%" for "the first 3 months after the registration day" and "50%" for "any other time during the first 6 months".
-- Months are counted here as whole months completed since the registration day.
@export How much income compensation do I get after my election to seek damages is registered (s 423)?
GIVEN `my assessed whole of person impairment` IS A NUMBER @desc Your degree of permanent whole of person impairment, in per cent, as assessed by an approved assessor
      `whole months since registration` IS A NUMBER @desc Whole months completed since the day the election was registered
      `what I would otherwise get` IS A NUMBER @desc The income compensation you would get without s 423
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much income compensation do I get after my election` MEANS
    IF `my assessed whole of person impairment` AT LEAST 25
    THEN `what I would otherwise get`
    ELSE IF `whole months since registration` LESS THAN 3
         THEN `what I would otherwise get` TIMES 70 DIVIDED BY 100
         ELSE IF `whole months since registration` LESS THAN 6
              THEN `what I would otherwise get` TIMES 50 DIVIDED BY 100
              ELSE 0

@export Does my registered election end compensation for medical expenses I incur afterwards (s 423(1)(d))?
GIVEN `my assessed whole of person impairment` IS A NUMBER @desc Your degree of permanent whole of person impairment, in per cent, as assessed by an approved assessor
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does my election end my medical expenses compensation` IF
    `my assessed whole of person impairment` LESS THAN 25
